1121:| OD6 | <a id="od6"></a>Traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity | D7; implemented by bug 186's repair (TRAIT-1 – TRAIT-9) | bug 186's repair (exit 0b), so every deliverable; R-0548, R-0549; V5 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity. Completed 2026-11-02: qualified as `Trait::method(&x, ...)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current qualification rule: this OD6 as completed by the audit below; the audit supplies syntax, not a second rule.** |
1125:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
1127:| OD12 | <a id="od12"></a>Contracts (H27): a pure, non-consuming specification judgment, with machine and mathematical arithmetic kept distinct | T-H27 and §4.5; pending (R-0473) | R-0473 (row 12); any V1a or V1b claim stated with a contract | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a contract is a pure, non-consuming specification judgment, with machine and mathematical arithmetic kept distinct |
1160:| OD45 | <a id="od45"></a>Capability-variable inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 063's ruling). Main today binds a callee's `cap C` variable from the capability set of an argument's function type when its type peek (`PeekType`) can compute that type without checking the argument, and refuses a variable no argument determines (E0242, `pick_generic::<i64>()`). Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the argument forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) require an explicit capability argument whenever an argument is not a name. Recommendation: (b), so whether a call infers does not depend on the argument's spelling Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) bind from the checked type of every argument; refuse unresolved or inconsistent ones; no result-context inference | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TRUST-4, TYPE-1; bug 063's record | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Bind from checked type of every argument; no result-context. **Revised by the owner 2026-11-05 (row Q5.1/Q5.4, [revision](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked; no capability inference** |
1161:| OD46 | <a id="od46"></a>Generic type-argument inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 308's ruling). Main today binds each type parameter from the types its peek (`PeekType`) computes for the arguments given for it, a value `if` or `match` included since bug 308, but answers `.placeholder` for a generic call's result and some arms with binders, and refuses a parameter no argument determines; it never infers from the expected result type. Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) also infer from the expected type at the call site. Recommendation: (b), local and predictable, with explicit type arguments for the rest; Austral infers type parameters from the arguments, and a `let` declaration's type can disambiguate a return-type-polymorphic call Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) from checked argument types only, never from the expected result; arguments that need an expected type are refused, not defaulted | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TYPE-13, TYPE-15; bug 308's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): From checked argument types only |
1175:| OD60 | <a id="od60"></a>One consuming receiver form for `Destroy` (added 2026-10-30, item S8 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Destroy` is implemented through one consuming receiver form only. Options: (a) keep the receiver forms the current compiler accepts; (b) one consuming form (`fn destroy(self)`) only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) one consuming form. Austral: no destructor trait; a linear type's module exports an ordinary consuming function | a `Destroy` impl may take `self` or `&self`; `&mut self` is refused (E0587) | OWN-13, TRAIT-9, TRAIT-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One consuming form |
1176:| OD61 | <a id="od61"></a>One coordinated removal list (added 2026-10-30, item review new 7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the decided and proposed removals (inline `mod`, the C-style `for`, `if let` / `while let` / `let ... else`, `newtype`, `..base`, range patterns, guards, `in R`, `break <expr>` and the `trusted mod` marker) are migrated as one coordinated list. Options: (a) one list, migrated in exit 2q's single transition; (b) each removal on its own schedule. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) one list, including dropping TRUST-6's `trusted mod` marker, which S14 removes (S14 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it). Austral: not applicable | TRUST-6 (pending) still requires the `trusted mod` marker, which S14's pick would drop (no owner answer is recorded for S14) | TRUST-6, STMT-6, PAT-6, PAT-7, PAT-4, TYPE-7, MOD-4, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One list (incl. dropping `trusted mod`) |
1177:| OD62 | <a id="od62"></a>Unused values and variables (added 2026-10-30, item Q3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether an unused local variable or parameter is refused (Go's rule) and whether an unused `Result` or `Option` may be dropped by `discard`. Options: (a) refuse only an unused `Result` or `Option` (OWN-6); (b) Go's rule: any unused local or parameter is refused (an intentionally unused parameter is named `_`), and `discard` refuses `Result` and `Option`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b), stated as one invariant: every name a body binds is used or is `_` (a bodyless declaration has no body, so needs no exception); `discard` and wildcard drops refuse `Result` and `Option`, Copy or not; to clarify: `_` as a loop index, and an unused `&mut` binding under REF-10. Austral: not checked | unused Copy locals and parameters are accepted; OWN-6 refuses an unused fallible result | OWN-5, OWN-6, OWN-7, REF-10 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Every bound name used or `_`; `discard` refuses Result/Option |
1178:| OD63 | <a id="od63"></a>Float literals take their expected type (added 2026-10-30, item Q4 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a float literal takes the float type its position expects and is refused where nothing is expected, as ARITH-2@2 states under OD31's note that float literals get an explicit rule under the same principle. Options: (a) yes, as ARITH-2@2 states for integers and floats alike; (b) a float literal defaults to `f64`. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a). Austral: not checked | `let x = 1.5;` is accepted as `Float64` (ARITH-2@2 is partial, bug 327) | ARITH-2, ARITH-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Expected type, refused where none |
1179:| OD64 | <a id="od64"></a>Raw pointers derived from a borrow (added 2026-10-30, item G19 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What a raw pointer derived from a borrow may do: whether it is valid only while the borrow lives, may escape the borrow block, may be passed to C, and how extern parameters say so. Options: (a) valid only while the borrow lives and never escaping it; (b) the operational loan rules of REF-14 only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): no returning, no escaping storage (a local `let` inside the block stays allowed), no converting to an integer and no capturing in an outer `defer`; borrow-derived pointers go only to call-only `extern` parameters; C uses it only during a call (memory C keeps must come from an allocation with its own owner); `extern` parameters say call-only (the default) or kept; field pointers come from the same borrow of the whole owner; deferred calls in the block run before the borrow ends; provenance survives copies, offsets, field pointers, helpers and indirect calls; a shared borrow never yields write access; `ptr::expose` is refused on such a pointer; the annotation's syntax gets an LL(1) check. Austral: not checked | no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity (OD15); REF-14 bounds aliases by their loan | REF-14, TRUST-1, TRUST-3, FFI-3, OWN-12 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Valid only inside the borrow; call-only extern params |
1180:| OD65 | <a id="od65"></a>Empty structs (added 2026-10-30, item review new 1 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a struct with no fields (`struct S {}`) is allowed, as a unit-like type for capability and root tokens, or refused. Options: (a) allowed; (b) refused. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | the parser accepts `struct S {}` and `S {}`; the grammar requires at least one field | TYPE-7, TYPE-16, TRUST-9, OPEN-38 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Allow |
1181:| OD66 | <a id="od66"></a>The unit literal `()` (added 2026-10-30, item review new 2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `()` is the unit value's literal, given that the grammar has no production for it. Options: (a) add `()` to the grammar; (b) no unit literal, Unit produced only by a statement or a Unit call. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: the unit value is written `nil` | the grammar has no production for `()` | SYN-1, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Add `()` |
1182:| OD67 | <a id="od67"></a>Locals that reuse a top-level name (added 2026-10-30, item review new 4 (SCOPE-4) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether a local binding may reuse the name of a top-level function, an import alias or an intrinsic (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.4 also lists constants). Options: (a) allowed, as today; (b) refused, as SCOPE-1 refuses shadowing a local. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | allowed | SCOPE-1, SCOPE-2, MOD-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
1183:| OD68 | <a id="od68"></a>Failure inside a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, item review new 5 of the owner picks of that day). Question: What OWN-12's text says about a failure inside a deferred call, given that an abort runs no deferred call (STMT-1, STMT-2). Options: (a) OWN-12 states that a trap inside a deferred call ends the program and runs no further deferred call, as STMT-2 and OD10 say for every abort; (b) the remaining deferred calls still run after a trapping one, which would amend STMT-2 and OD10. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: not checked | failure is abort only (STMT-2); OWN-12's rationale leaves the case open | OWN-12, STMT-1, STMT-2, STMT-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Trap ends the program, no further deferred call |
1184:| OD69 | <a id="od69"></a>What "the remaining locals are destroyed" means (added 2026-10-30, item review new 6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether STMT-11's last exit step ("the remaining locals are destroyed") means storage teardown only, with no synthesized `Destroy` call, as OWN-13 requires. Options: (a) storage teardown only, no call, as OWN-13 requires; (b) a remaining local whose type implements `Destroy` is destroyed by a synthesized call, which would amend OWN-13. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (raised by the review). Austral: no implicit destruction | nothing is destroyed implicitly (OWN-13) | STMT-11, OWN-13 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Storage teardown only |
1192:| OD77 | <a id="od77"></a>Resync the grammar to the decided language (added 2026-10-30, item B of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `grammar/concrete.ebnf` is resynchronized for the items the language already decided: `E#V` becomes `E::V`, binding renaming, a function body with no trailing value and `;` for bodyless functions, no generic trait methods, assignment targets are places, unknown attribute names refused, and no `break <expr>`. Options: (a) approve the resync, as actual productions with a factored `place` prefix for assignment; (b) defer it, the divergences staying recorded in grammar/conformance_known.tsv (the decided language does not change either way). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) approve, with actual productions, a `place` prefix and `break <expr>` removed. Austral: not applicable | the grammar is stale on these items (grammar/conformance_known.tsv) | SYN-1, PAT-1, STMT-6, STMT-8, OWN-8, FFI-6 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Approve resync with actual productions |
1194:| OD79 | <a id="od79"></a>`assert` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-annotation statement `assert expr;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add `assert`. Extended 2026-11-06 by `assert(e) by thm(...)` ([SPARK row](#decided-project-spark-2026-11-06)) |
1197:| OD82 | <a id="od82"></a>Loop contract attributes and where attributes attach (added 2026-10-30, item C5 (R7) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains `#[invariant(e)]` and `#[variant(e)]` before a loop, and whether attributes attach by kind (declaration attributes to the next declaration, loop attributes to the next loop). Options: (a) add them to the grammar and attach attributes by kind; (b) remove them from the parser; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): attach by kind, declaration attributes to the next declaration, `#[invariant]` and `#[variant]` to the next loop. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts them | CON-2, STMT-12, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add; attributes attach by kind **Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `#[variant]` also attaches to a function, one measure per recursive cycle; `#[may_not_terminate("reason")]` also attaches as a prefix to an expression (an indirect call), through one attribute-prefix production in statements.** |
1206:| OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` |
1214:| OD99 | <a id="od99"></a>The names of the named conversions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: The spellings of the named conversions of ARITH-5, ARITH-10 and ARITH-11 (`try_convert`, `truncate`, `reinterpret`, the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions, and the float roundings `trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`). Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10, ARITH-11. The current compiler: none of them exists (E0101). ARITH-5's sentence names `truncate` and `reinterpret`, while the archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 calls its spellings indicative, to be fixed by the API's owner; it names the integer-to-float and `f64`-to-`f32` conversions by their rounding only. Options: (a) the spellings above, as builtins; (b) other names, or std functions rather than builtins. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-99 | see OPEN-99 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): These spellings, as builtins |
1215:| OD100 | <a id="od100"></a>Comparisons on a type parameter (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a comparison on a value of a `Copy`-bounded type parameter is accepted and checked again at each instantiation, or refused in the generic body. Bears on TYPE-6, TYPE-9, PASS-1, OPEN-42. The current compiler: `a < b` with `a, b: T` and `T: Copy` is accepted, and an instantiation at a type with no comparison is refused after monomorphization (E0229, `post-mono`); without the `Copy` bound it is refused in the body (E0229, `check`). TYPE-9's rationale says a generic body is checked once, from its bounds, which a per-instantiation check departs from. Options: (a) keep the current compiler's per-instantiation check; (b) refuse a comparison on a type parameter until a bound states it (a comparison trait); (c) refuse it. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md §8a (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-100 | see OPEN-100 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse until a bound states it |
1216:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
1217:| OD102 | <a id="od102"></a>`Float32` and `Char` as type spellings (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether `Float32` and `Char` are other spellings of `f32` and `char`, as `Float64` and `Bool` are of `f64` and `bool`. Bears on TYPE-19, SYN-3. The current compiler: the parser maps `Float64` and `Bool` to `f64` and `bool` but not `Float32` or `Char`, so `fn g(x: f32) -> Float32 { return x; }` is refused (E0220) while the resolver's `tyFromName` maps all four. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §1 names `Float32`/`f32` as two spellings. Options: (a) `Float32` and `Char` become spellings of `f32` and `char`; (b) neither, keeping the current compiler's `Float64` and `Bool`; (c) `Float32` and `Char` decided separately. Whether every capitalized spelling (`Int`, `Uint`, `Float64`, `Bool`) is removed is a separate removal question, not asked here. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-102 | see OPEN-102 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `Float32` or `Char` aliases. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: the aliases `Int`, `Uint`, `Float64` and `Bool` are removed too ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-aliases)) |
1218:| OD103 | <a id="od103"></a>`as` between reference types (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing MEMORY_SEMANTICS). Question: Whether `as` may convert a reference type to a reference type, given that REF-5 lists borrowing and projecting as the only operations that form a reference. Bears on REF-5, REF-6, ARITH-5, TRUST-4. The current compiler: an identity cast between equal reference types is accepted and denotes the operand's place (`read(r as &Int)` with `r: &Int`, and `read(&v as &Int)`, exit 42 on both consumers); every other cast between reference types, `&mut Int as &Int` included, is E0553 at core-check under ARITH-5's tag, whose sentence covers only numeric, `bool` and `char` conversions; a cast from a reference to a raw pointer is allowed (TRUST-4). The design record (MEMORY_SEMANTICS §15.3) listed a cast of a traced reference binding (`r as &U`) among the reference-forming operations. Options: (a) refuse `as` between reference types; (b) allow the identity cast as a no-op on the operand's place. Conversions between a reference and a raw pointer are OPEN-98's, not this question's. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the MEMORY_SEMANTICS absorption, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-103 | see OPEN-103 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse |
1219:| OD104 | <a id="od104"></a>A `#[variant]` written on a counted loop (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the OD47 loop rules). Question: whether a `#[variant]` attribute written on a counted loop is refused, checked like any loop variant, or ignored. Options: (a) refused, the loop's own measure being its only variant; (b) allowed and checked as a loop variant (CON-2); (c) allowed and ignored. No pick given | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |
1220:| OD105 | <a id="od105"></a>Where a counted loop's `#[invariant]` is checked (added 2026-10-30, from the same review). Question: where an `#[invariant]` on a counted loop is checked, given that CON-2 checks a loop invariant before the loop and after every iteration. Options: (a) as CON-2 says for every loop; (b) at each entry to the body, so nothing is checked when the loop runs no iteration. No pick given; (b) needs proof requirement 2 | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): As CON-2: before the loop and after every iteration |
1221:| OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1222:| OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
1223:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
1224:| OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
1230:| OD115 | <a id="od115"></a>The body and totality of a specification function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a `spec fn` may be declared without a body, and whether its body must be shown total. Bears on CON-3, CON-5, CON-1. The current compiler: both `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool;` and `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool = x > 10;` are accepted, and so is a recursive body that never terminates (`spec fn loopy(x: Int) -> bool = loopy(x + 1);`), with no check. [CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md) calls a `spec fn` "an erased mathematical specification, no body"; contracts.md's examples (CON-3) give it a body; [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) requires it to be total. Options: (a) a body is required and must be total (structural recursion or a checked measure); (b) a body is optional, a body-less one an uninterpreted function a proof must constrain, and a body must be total; (c) as in the current compiler, no totality check. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-115 | see OPEN-115 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Body required and total |
1235:| OD120 | <a id="od120"></a>A vacuous contract (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a contract whose precondition is unsatisfiable is refused by the language or only reported, under a project policy. Bears on CON-1, CON-2. The current compiler: `#[requires(x > 5 && x < 3)]` compiles; a decided-unsatisfiable precondition makes the claim `vacuous`, never `proved`, and E0613 refuses it only under the `forbid-vacuous` policy (`Concrete.Policy`), not in a plain build. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) states the reporting rule. Options: (a) a language refusal in every build; (b) tooling only: always reported `vacuous`, refused under a policy, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-120 | see OPEN-120 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Tooling: reported `vacuous`, refused under policy **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a vacuous precondition used for safety admission is refused in every build.** |
1236:| OD121 | <a id="od121"></a>Projections from a base that is not a traceable place (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a field access, index or `*` whose base is not a traceable place (an `if` or `match` value, `**r`, a reference read out of storage) is refused, or accepted once the checker can trace it. Bears on REF-5, MEM-4, MEM-8, TYPE-7. The current compiler: refused (E0238, `untraceablePlace`), whether the projection reads a Copy value or forms a reference: `(if c { a } else { b }).f`, `(**r).f`, `*w.r`. No entry states the refusal: REF-5 forbids a branch value or a stored reference as a reference's root, and MEM-4, MEM-8 and TYPE-7 do not restrict a projection's base. [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) H28 calls it a deliberate restriction until R-0499's checker on Core. Options: (a) state the restriction as a rule (a projection's base is a place: a binding, its fields and elements, or `*` of a named reference); (b) accept such projections of owned values and Copy reads once the checker traces them, refusing only what REF-5 refuses. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-121 | see OPEN-121 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): State the restriction as a rule |
1237:| OD122 | <a id="od122"></a>A `repr(C)` struct returned from an extern function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[repr(C)]` struct returned from an `extern fn` is returned as the platform's C calling convention returns it, at every size. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-11. The current compiler: a returned struct of at most 8 bytes comes back as one 64-bit integer, stored as 8 bytes into the struct's slot even when the struct is smaller (a 4-byte `in_addr` returned by `inet_makeaddr` reads back wrong natively); a larger one is returned as an LLVM aggregate, which matches C on x86-64 and AArch64 only for some layouts. [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) promised returns of at most 8 bytes and listed larger ones as not promised. Options: (a) every size, as C, classified by the `Layout` authority (R-0502); (b) at most 8 bytes (or 16), a larger return refused in an `extern fn` signature; (c) none, a returned struct going through an out-pointer the binding declares. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-122 | see OPEN-122 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Every size per C ABI via `Layout` |
1238:| OD123 | <a id="od123"></a>`as` on a newtype outside its module (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether `v as N` and `n as T`, between a newtype `N` and its inner type `T`, are accepted outside the module that declares `N`, where TYPE-11 refuses `N(v)`. Bears on TYPE-11, TYPE-24, ARITH-5, OPEN-54. The current compiler: both accepted in any module, so `x as Id` builds a foreign newtype that `Id(x)` may not (E0296), and a validated wrapper's smart constructor can be bypassed. [VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md) §8 describes the cast rule as guarding the wrapper contract from an `as` bypass, which holds for casts to unrelated types only. Options: (a) accepted anywhere, as in the current compiler; (b) accepted only where `N(v)` and `.0` are (TYPE-11, with R-0500's follow-on for `.0`); (c) `as` never names a newtype, the conversions being `N(v)` and `.0`. Austral: no newtype (OPEN-54's comparison). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-123 | see OPEN-123 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype |
1239:| OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
1240:| OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.** |
1241:| OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles |
1242:| OD127 | <a id="od127"></a>UTF-8 in every `String` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether every `String` value that safe code can reach holds valid UTF-8, as a language guarantee. Bears on TYPE-3, SYN-5, TRUST-2. The current compiler: literals are UTF-8 (SYN-5), but nothing keeps a `String` valid: `push_char` appends a byte unchecked, `Bytes::to_string` transfers bytes without validation, and std's `read_to_string` does not validate. [STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md) §1 states the invariant as a target, and [UNICODE_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/UNICODE_POLICY.md) has a validating `Bytes::to_string` returning `Option`. Options: (a) a language guarantee, every safe constructor validating and an unchecked one being a trusted operation with that premise; (b) a std convention only, `String` being bytes to the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-127 | see OPEN-127 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Language guarantee |
1243:| OD128 | <a id="od128"></a>`->` between a match arm's pattern and body (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a match arm may separate its pattern from its body with `->` as well as `=>`. Bears on SYN-1 and PAT-7. The current compiler: the parser accepts both (`parseMatchArms`; `2 -> { return 42; }` exits 42 on both consumers), and the grammar's `match_arm` has only `=>`. The syntax freeze review (2026-04, [SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md) §1.5) recommended `=>` as the canonical form with a warning for `->`, which also spells a return type. Options: (a) `=>` only, `->` refused; (b) both accepted; (c) both accepted, with a warning on `->`, as the freeze review recommended. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-128 | see OPEN-128 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `=>` only |
1244:| OD129 | <a id="od129"></a>Type arguments of an enum literal taken from its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an enum literal or variant written without type arguments (`Option::Some { value: 42 }`, `Option::None`) takes them from the type its position expects, and from which positions. Bears on TYPE-7, TYPE-15 and OPEN-46, which asks the same of a generic call's type arguments and whose pick excludes the expected result type. The current compiler: the type arguments come from the expected type of a `return` (the function's result type), of an annotated `let` and of a call argument (the parameter's type); all three exit 42 on both consumers, and spec examples rely on it (PAT-1). The syntax freeze review (§1.3, kill-list items 1, 4 and 5) proposed this inference for enum literals and for constructor calls such as `Vec::new()`. Options: (a) explicit type arguments always; (b) from the expected type in the positions the current compiler uses; (c) the answer to OPEN-46, applied to enum literals. Austral: type parameters are inferred from the arguments, and a declaration's type can disambiguate. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-129 | see OPEN-129 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Same rule as OD46: type arguments from the arguments, otherwise explicit (`None::<i32>`) |
1260:| OD145 | <a id="od145"></a>How a contract names machine arithmetic (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: How a contract expression names machine arithmetic, which CON-3 allows in place of its default mathematical arithmetic. Bears on CON-3 and ARITH-4. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == wrapping_add(x, 1))]` is accepted and the program runs (contracts are erased at run time); what the proof pipeline makes of the named operation was not probed. CON-3 says a contract's integer arithmetic is mathematical unless it names a machine operation, and no rule or example names one. Options: (a) the named operations of ARITH-4 (`wrapping_add`, `saturating_add` and the rest) are the machine operations in a contract; (b) a contract-only spelling; (c) contracts have only mathematical arithmetic, and CON-3 drops the exception. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-145 | see OPEN-145 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): ARITH-4 named operations |
