1151:| OD36 | <a id="od36"></a>The spelling of the explicit non-consuming place form that opens a `Heap` or `HeapArray` (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD30, which requires one and keeps `*` as the consuming move-out). Options: a borrow through the cell (`(&h).f`, `(&mut h).f`), a named projection (`h.open().f`), or a dedicated operator. Recommendation: the borrow form, which needs no new syntax and matches OD32's explicit receiver borrow Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only through a borrow block (`borrow h as r { r.f }`), no `(&h).f`; specify the result type for `Heap<T>`, `&Heap<T>` and `&mut Heap<T>`, one heap layer per block (MEM-4), and `HeapArray` elements | the current compiler opens one heap layer implicitly (`h.f` on `Heap<S>`, bug 326) | bug 326's repair (MEM-4) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only through a borrow block; no `(&h).f` |
1174:| OD59 | <a id="od59"></a>One way to consume a `Heap` (added 2026-10-30, item S7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Which operations consume a `Heap<T>`: the move-out `*h` (OD30), `destroy(h)`, `free(h)` or a new `take(h)`. Options: (a) keep `*h` and `free(h)` as today; (b) `*h` as move-out-and-free and `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, `free` deleted, no `take`; (c) `take(h)` replaces `*h` as the move-out, `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, `free` deleted. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b): `*h` and a `destroy(h)` extended to a Heap, no `free`, no `take` (a `take` would be another spelling of `*h`). Austral: not checked | `*h` moves out and frees and `free(h)` frees the cell and returns its value (MEM-5); `destroy(h)` on a Heap is refused (E0272: destroy() requires a named type) | MEM-5, OWN-13, OPEN-36 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `*h` + `destroy(h)`, no `free`, no `take` |
1175:| OD60 | <a id="od60"></a>One consuming receiver form for `Destroy` (added 2026-10-30, item S8 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Destroy` is implemented through one consuming receiver form only. Options: (a) keep the receiver forms the current compiler accepts; (b) one consuming form (`fn destroy(self)`) only. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) one consuming form. Austral: no destructor trait; a linear type's module exports an ordinary consuming function | a `Destroy` impl may take `self` or `&self`; `&mut self` is refused (E0587) | OWN-13, TRAIT-9, TRAIT-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One consuming form |
